**Goal.**  
Show that for a function \(f:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z\)

\[
\bigl(\forall a\,b,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)\bigr)
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
\Bigl(\forall z,\;f(z)=0\Bigr)\;\lor\;
\Bigl(\exists c,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c\Bigr).
\]

The proof is split into two directions.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  From the functional equation to the two possibilities  

Assume  

\[
\tag{H}\forall a\,b,\qquad f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr).          \tag{1}
\]

---

#### 1.1  First consequences of (1)

1. Put \(a=0\) in (1):
   \[
   f(0)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(b)\bigr).\tag{2}
   \]

2. Put \(b=0\) in (1):
   \[
   f(2a)+2f(0)=f\bigl(f(a)\bigr).\tag{3}
   \]

3. In (2) replace \(b\) by \(a\) and compare with (3):
   \[
   f(0)+2f(a)=f\bigl(f(a)\bigr)=f(2a)+2f(0).
   \]
   Hence  

   \[
   \boxed{\,f(2a)=2f(a)-f(0)\,}\qquad\text{for every }a\in\mathbb Z.\tag{4}
   \]

4. Using (2) we can rewrite (1) as  

   \[
   f(2a)+2f(b)=f(0)+2f(a+b).\tag{5}
   \]

   Substituting (4) for \(f(2a)\) gives  

   \[
   2f(a)-f(0)+2f(b)=f(0)+2f(a+b),
   \]
   i.e.  

   \[
   \boxed{\,f(f(a+b))=2f(a)+2f(b)-f(0)\,}.\tag{6}
   \]

5. Take \(b=-a\) in (6).  Since \(a+(-a)=0\), the left‑hand side is \(f(f(0))\).  
   From (2) with \(b=0\) we have \(f(f(0))=f(0)+2f(0)=3f(0)\).  
   Consequently  

   \[
   3f(0)=2f(a)+2f(-a)-f(0)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
   \boxed{\,f(a)+f(-a)=2f(0)\,}\tag{7}
   \]

   for all \(a\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 1.2  Two cases according to the value of \(k:=f(0)\)

Let \(k:=f(0)\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Case A.** \(k=0\).

* From (4) we obtain \(f(2a)=2f(a)\).  
* From (7) we obtain \(f(-a)=-f(a)\); thus \(f\) is odd.  

Insert \(k=0\) into (5).  Using \(f(2a)=2f(a)\) we get  

\[
2f(a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr).\tag{8}
\]

But (2) with \(k=0\) reads \(f\bigl(f(x)\bigr)=2f(x)\) for every \(x\).  
Applying this to \(x=a+b\) in (8) yields  

\[
2f(a+b)=2f(a)+2f(b)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{\,f(a+b)=f(a)+f(b)\,}\tag{9}
\]

so \(f\) is additive on \(\mathbb Z\).

Additivity together with \(f(2a)=2f(a)\) forces \(f\) to be *linear*:
for every integer \(n\),

\[
f(n)=n\,f(1).\tag{10}
\]

Write \(c:=f(1)\); then \(f(z)=c\,z\) for all \(z\).

Plug this expression into the original equation (1):

\[
c(2a)+2c b = c\bigl(c(a+b)\bigr)
\;\Longrightarrow\;
2c(a+b)=c^{2}(a+b).
\]

Since \(a+b\) can be any integer, we must have  

\[
c^{2}=2c\quad\Longrightarrow\quad c=0\ \text{or}\ c=2.
\]

*If \(c=0\)*, then \(f(z)=0\) for all \(z\); this is the first alternative.

*If \(c=2\)*, then \(f(z)=2z\); this is the second alternative with the constant \(c=0\).

Thus, when \(f(0)=0\) we have proved  

\[
\bigl(\forall a\,b,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f(f(a+b))\bigr)
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\bigl(\forall z,\;f(z)=0\bigr)\;\lor\;
\bigl(\exists c,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c\bigr).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Case B.** \(k\neq 0\).

Define a new function  

\[
g(z):=f(z)-k\qquad(z\in\mathbb Z).
\]

Then \(g(0)=0\).  Using (4) and (7) we compute

\[
\begin{aligned}
g(2a)&=f(2a)-k
      =\bigl(2f(a)-k\bigr)-k
      =2\bigl(f(a)-k\bigr)=2g(a),\\[2mm]
g(-a)&=f(-a)-k
      =\bigl(2k-f(a)\bigr)-k
      =k-f(a) =-g(a).
\end{aligned}
\tag{11}
\]

Hence \(g\) is also odd and satisfies \(g(2a)=2g(a)\).

Now rewrite (1) in terms of \(g\).  
Using (2) with the notation \(k=f(0)\),

\[
f\bigl(f(x)\bigr)=k+2f(x)=2g(x)+3k.
\]

Consequently,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{LHS of (1)}&=f(2a)+2f(b)
                 =\bigl(g(2a)+k\bigr)+2\bigl(g(b)+k\bigr) \\
                &=g(2a)+2g(b)+3k,\\
\text{RHS of (1)}&=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)
                 =2g(a+b)+3k .
\end{aligned}
\]

Cancelling the common term \(3k\) we obtain  

\[
g(2a)+2g(b)=2g(a+b).\tag{12}
\]

Insert (11) for \(g(2a)\) (i.e. \(g(2a)=2g(a)\)) into (12); after dividing by \(2\),

\[
g(a)+g(b)=g(a+b).\tag{13}
\]

Thus \(g\) is additive and \(g(0)=0\); by the same argument as in Case A we get

\[
g(z)=c\,z\qquad\text{for some integer }c.\tag{14}
\]

Returning to \(f\),

\[
f(z)=g(z)+k=c\,z+k.\tag{15}
\]

Insert (15) into the original functional equation (1).  A straightforward calculation gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{LHS}&=c(2a)+k+2\bigl(c b+k\bigr)=2c a+2c b+3k,\\
\text{RHS}&=c\bigl(c(a+b)+k\bigr)+k=c^{2}(a+b)+c k+k.
\end{aligned}
\]

Equality for all integers \(a,b\) forces the coefficients of \(a+b\) and the constant term to match:

\[
\begin{cases}
2c = c^{2},\\[1mm]
3k = (c+1)k .
\end{cases}
\tag{16}
\]

From the first equation \(c(c-2)=0\), so \(c=0\) or \(c=2\).

*If \(c=0\)*, the second equation becomes \(3k= k\), i.e. \(2k=0\).  
Because we are in the case \(k\neq 0\), this is impossible.  
Hence \(c=0\) forces \(k=0\), which brings us back to Case A and yields the
identically‑zero solution.

*If \(c=2\)*, the second equation reads \(3k=3k\), which holds for every integer \(k\).  
Therefore  

\[
f(z)=2z+k\qquad(z\in\mathbb Z)
\]

with an arbitrary constant \(k\).

Summarising, when \(k\neq0\) the only functions satisfying (1) are the
functions \(f(z)=2z+k\).  Together with the identically‑zero function we have proved

\[
\bigl(\forall a\,b,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f(f(a+b))\bigr)
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\bigl(\forall z,\;f(z)=0\bigr)\;\lor\;
\bigl(\exists c,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c\bigr).
\tag{17}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The converse implications  

*If \(f(z)=0\) for all \(z\)*, then both sides of (1) are equal to \(0\); the
equation holds.

*If \(f(z)=2z+c\) for a fixed integer \(c\)*, then

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{LHS}&=f(2a)+2f(b)
          =(4a+c)+2(2b+c)=4a+4b+3c,\\
\text{RHS}&=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)
          =f\bigl(2(a+b)+c\bigr)
          =2\bigl(2(a+b)+c\bigr)+c=4a+4b+3c,
\end{aligned}
\]

so (1) is satisfied.

Thus each of the two alternatives indeed implies the functional equation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Conclusion  

We have shown

\[
\bigl(\forall a\,b,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f(f(a+b))\bigr)
\iff
\Bigl(\forall z,\;f(z)=0\Bigr)\;\lor\;
\Bigl(\exists c,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c\Bigr),
\]

which is exactly the statement of the theorem. ∎

</informal_proof>
