Description: The tactic `split_ifs` decomposes all if-then-else expressions in the goal into separate subgoals. For a goal of the form `g (if p then x else y)`, it produces two subgoals: one assuming `p` and proving `g x`, and another assuming `¬p` and proving `g y`. When multiple if-then-else expressions are present, it splits them starting from the top-most expression whose condition doesn't contain another if-then-else. The tactic can also be applied to hypotheses using `split_ifs at *`, and allows custom naming of the introduced hypotheses with `split_ifs with h₁ h₂ h₃`.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

5. [DEFINITION] Peeling a quantifier from a biconditional
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.Peel
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.Peel.peelIffAux
Description: The tactic peels off a single quantifier from a biconditional statement (`↔`), applying appropriate congruence lemmas for universal quantifiers (`forall_congr'`), existential quantifiers (`exists_congr`), filter operations (`eventually_congr`, `frequently_congr`), or implications (`and_congr_right`). If no suitable lemma applies, it fails with the message "failed to apply a quantifier congruence lemma."
Signature:  : TacticM Unit

