**Goal.**  
From  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{35}\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{5k\pi}{180}\Bigr)=\tan\!\Bigl(\frac{m\pi}{180}\Bigr) \tag{1}
\]

together with  

\[
0<m,\qquad \frac{m.\text{num}}{m.\text{den}}<90, \tag{2}
\]

prove  

\[
\uparrow m.\text{den}+m.\text{num}=177 . \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A closed form for the left‑hand side of (1)

Let  

\[
\theta:=\frac{5\pi}{180}= \frac{\pi}{36}.
\]

For any real \(\theta\neq 0\) and any natural \(n\) we have the well‑known identity  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{n}\sin(k\theta)=\frac{\sin\!\bigl(\frac{n\theta}{2}\bigr)\,
      \sin\!\bigl(\frac{(n+1)\theta}{2}\bigr)}{\sin\!\bigl(\frac{\theta}{2}\bigr)} .
\tag{4}
\]

(Identity (4) is proved by writing each \(\sin(k\theta)\) as the imaginary part of
\(e^{ik\theta}\) and summing the resulting geometric series.)

Take \(n=35\) and \(\theta=\pi/36\).  Substituting these values in (4) gives  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{35}\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{5k\pi}{180}\Bigr)
   =\frac{\sin\!\bigl(\frac{35\theta}{2}\bigr)\,
          \sin\!\bigl(\frac{36\theta}{2}\bigr)}{\sin\!\bigl(\frac{\theta}{2}\bigr)} .
\tag{5}
\]

Compute the three sines appearing in (5):

* \(\displaystyle\frac{35\theta}{2}= \frac{35}{2}\cdot\frac{\pi}{36}
      =\frac{35\pi}{72}= \frac{87.5^{\circ}\pi}{180}\).
  Hence  

  \[
  \sin\!\Bigl(\frac{35\theta}{2}\Bigr)=\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{35\pi}{72}\Bigr)
   =\cos\!\Bigl(\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{35\pi}{72}\Bigr)
   =\cos\!\Bigl(\frac{\pi}{72}\Bigr).
  \tag{6}
  \]

* \(\displaystyle\frac{36\theta}{2}= \frac{36}{2}\cdot\frac{\pi}{36}= \frac{\pi}{2}\).
  Therefore  

  \[
  \sin\!\Bigl(\frac{36\theta}{2}\Bigr)=\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{\pi}{2}\Bigr)=1 .
  \tag{7}
  \]

* \(\displaystyle\frac{\theta}{2}= \frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{\pi}{36}= \frac{\pi}{72}\).
  Hence  

  \[
  \sin\!\Bigl(\frac{\theta}{2}\Bigr)=\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{\pi}{72}\Bigr).
  \tag{8}
  \]

Insert (6)–(8) into (5):

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{35}\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{5k\pi}{180}\Bigr)
   =\frac{\cos(\pi/72)\cdot 1}{\sin(\pi/72)}
   =\frac{\cos(\pi/72)}{\sin(\pi/72)} .
\tag{9}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Rewriting (9) as a tangent

Using the identity \(\displaystyle\frac{\cos\alpha}{\sin\alpha}
          =\cot\alpha =\tan\!\bigl(\frac{\pi}{2}-\alpha\bigr)\) we obtain  

\[
\frac{\cos(\pi/72)}{\sin(\pi/72)}
   =\tan\!\Bigl(\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{\pi}{72}\Bigr)
   =\tan\!\Bigl(\frac{35\pi}{72}\Bigr). \tag{10}
\]

Recall that \(\displaystyle\frac{35\pi}{72}= \frac{175\pi}{360}
      =\frac{175}{2}\cdot\frac{\pi}{180}\).
Thus (10) can be written as  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{35}\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{5k\pi}{180}\Bigr)
   =\tan\!\Bigl(\frac{175}{2}\,\frac{\pi}{180}\Bigr). \tag{11}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Identification of the rational number \(m\)

Equation (1) tells us that the same sum equals \(\tan\!\bigl(\frac{m\pi}{180}\bigr)\).
Combining (1) with (11) we have  

\[
\tan\!\Bigl(\frac{m\pi}{180}\Bigr)=\tan\!\Bigl(\frac{175}{2}\,
                                          \frac{\pi}{180}\Bigr). \tag{12}
\]

Because of (2) we know  

\[
0<m<90 .
\]

Consequently \(\displaystyle 0<\frac{m\pi}{180}<\frac{\pi}{2}\);
the same strict inequality holds for \(\displaystyle
\frac{175}{2}\,\frac{\pi}{180}= \frac{35\pi}{72}\) because
\(\frac{35\pi}{72}=87.5^{\circ}<90^{\circ}\).

On the interval \((0,\pi/2)\) the tangent function is **strictly increasing**,
hence it is injective there.  Therefore from (12) we may conclude  

\[
\frac{m\pi}{180}= \frac{175}{2}\,\frac{\pi}{180},
\qquad\text{and consequently}\qquad
m=\frac{175}{2}. \tag{13}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Numerator and denominator of \(m\)

The rational number \(\displaystyle\frac{175}{2}\) is already in lowest terms,
because \(\gcd(175,2)=1\).  By the definition of the canonical representation of a
rational number we have  

\[
m.\text{num}=175,\qquad m.\text{den}=2 .
\tag{14}
\]

(If one wishes to invoke a library lemma, it is exactly
`Rat.num_natCast` or `Rat.num_ofNat` after rewriting \(175\) as a natural
number.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Computation of \(\uparrow m.\text{den}+m.\text{num}\)

Finally,

\[
\uparrow m.\text{den}+m.\text{num}= \uparrow 2 + 175 = 2+175 = 177 .
\tag{15}
\]

Thus the required equality (3) holds.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Conclusion.**  
Under the hypotheses \(0<m\), the trigonometric identity (1) and the bound
\(\frac{m.\text{num}}{m.\text{den}}<90\), we have shown that \(m=175/2\) and
hence \(\uparrow m.\text{den}+m.\text{num}=177\). ∎

</informal_proof>
